[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the site DNS resolver at 10.5.0.4 (recursive DNS service/policy). Client hosts across the office (PC1, PC2, PC3) all point to 10.5.0.4 as their nameserver, and DNS lookups to it are answered with “REFUSED,” indicating recursion is disabled or blocked. That makes browsers “spin” because they cannot resolve external hostnames, even though L3 routing is fine.

Mechanism: From PC1 we can reach the default gateway and beyond; traceroute to 1.1.1.1 exits the office (10.1.0.2 → 10.0.0.45 → 10.0.0.33 → 203.0.113.1). Internal reachability between PCs also succeeds. PC1 can ping the DNS server (10.5.0.4), but nslookup to example.com returns “REFUSED” from 10.5.0.4. The same refusal is observed from PC2 and PC3, so the issue is not host-specific.

Localization: The problem is isolated to the DNS server/service at 10.5.0.4 (not listed as a managed device in this topology). Core and WAN routing devices (e.g., R1, CSW1/2) show normal paths toward the ISP, so the network itself is not the culprit.

Root cause: Recursive resolution was disabled or access control on the resolver was changed to deny queries from office subnets.

Corrective fix: Re-enable recursion and/or update the DNS ACLs on 10.5.0.4 to allow queries from campus subnets (e.g., 10.0.0.0/8 as appropriate). As a workaround, update DHCP option 6 or host resolv.conf to point clients to a working recursive DNS (internal or public) until the resolver configuration is corrected. === END ===